    "thinking": "We are in role G (integration). We have three source IDs to integrate: e0a08988bc0d4f08 (source A), 015bd08c24cf4419 (source B), and 8e2ef19488ab48a4 (source C). The current source is the baseline. Our goal is to produce a new src/solution.cpp that integrates transformations from these sources.\n\nWe need to examine each source's diff/transformations:\n\nSource A: \n- Replace std::cin/scanf with custom fast integer reader\n- Integrate horizontal prefix sum computation during input reading to remove mat storage (i.e., compute row-wise cumulative sums on the fly, store in a flat pref array)\n- Reduce block size from 256 to 64 for better cache locality\n- Add restrict pointers and ivdep pragma on inner search loop\n\nSource B:\n- Replace vector<vector<int>> with single contiguous vector<int> (row-major) for memory locality and SIMD access patterns\n- Implement integral image using two parallel passes: row-wise cumulative sums then vertical accumulation, both parallelized with OpenMP\n- Compute each submatrix sum in O(1) via four-corner formula from prefix array, eliminating loop-carried dependencies of original sliding window\n- Add #pragma omp simd to inner j-loop computing submatrix sums to encourage compiler vectorization\n- Use long long for prefix storage and intermediate computations; final result cast to int\n\nSource C:\n- algorithm change: integral image (prefix sum) with extra row/col of zeros, using nested loops without OpenMP? Actually source C uses #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum)\n- parallelization: OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction\n- reduction\n\nWe need to integrate the best parts. The goal is high performance on ARM64 with 4 cores, OMP_NUM_THREADS=4.\n\nFrom previous experiments (own_role_history), we see that combinations including source A's fast input and flattened integral image construction performed well (~0.013 seconds). Source B also had good results but slower? Actually source B alone was ~0.060 s, but when combined with others it improved. The best recent scores were around 0.909 (score) with mean_seconds ~0.0133.\n\nWe need to propose a new integration that likely combines:\n- Fast integer reader from A\n- Flattened integral image construction: either on-the-fly during input as in A, or two-pass as in B. The on-the-fly approach avoids storing the matrix separately and reduces memory traffic.\n- OpenMP parallelization for the vertical accumulation pass (as in B) to utilize multiple cores.\n- For the final search loop: use tiling with block size 64 from A, plus restrict pointers and ivdep pragma. Also consider using #pragma omp simd as in B? But note that ARM64 may not benefit much from SIMD pragmas; but we can include it.\n- Use long long for prefix sums to avoid overflow.\n\nWe should also consider the collapse(2) reduction approach from C, which parallelizes both i and j loops. However, with tiling (blocking), we need to be careful: if we tile outer loops, we cannot simply use collapse(2) because tiles are not independent? Actually we can still parallelize over tiles or over rows within a block. The best performing previous integration (experiment 42478fbfb3874c11) had score 0.9097 and mean_seconds 0.01335. That experiment used sources: fef0c26a621d4971, 5060afd492ee446e, 6d295b5f520242c0 (not our current set). But we have only these three.\n\nWe need to produce a new solution that integrates all three source ideas. Let's design:\n\n- Use custom fast integer reader from A.\n- Read input and simultaneously compute row-wise prefix sums into a flat array pref of size (n+1)*(n+1) as in A, but using long long for safety.\n- After reading, perform vertical accumulation pass parallelized with OpenMP over columns (as in B).\n- Then search: use tiling with block size 64 from A. For each tile, iterate i and j within the tile. Use restrict pointers on pref rows to help compiler. Add #pragma ivdep before inner loop.\n- Also add #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) over the outer i loop? But we have tiling; we can parallelize over tiles or over i blocks. In source A, they used nested loops without OpenMP on search (only restrict and ivdep). To utilize multiple cores, we should parallelize the search as well. We could use #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) over all i,j from 0 to n-s+1, but that would lose tiling. Alternatively, we can keep tiling and add OpenMP on the outer tile loop (ii). That is a good approach: parallelize over ii blocks.\n\n- Use long long for mxSum and final cast to int.\n- Ensure correctness: use four-corner formula with pref array indices correctly.\n\nLet's write code:\n\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nstatic int read_int() {\n    int x = 0;\n    char c = getchar_unlocked();\n    while (c < '0' || c > '9') {\n        if (c == '-') {\n            c = getchar_unlocked();\n            while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {\n                x = x * 10 - (c - '0');\n                c = getchar_unlocked();\n            }\n            return x;\n        }\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\n    }\n    do {\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\n    } while (c >= '0' && c <= '9');\n    return x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n = read_int();\n    int s = read_int();\n\n    const int np1 = n + 1;\n    std::vector<long long> pref(np1 * np1, 0LL);\n\n    // Read input and compute row-wise prefix sums on the fly\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        long long row_sum = 0;\n        const int base_i = (i + 1) * np1;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            int val = read_int();\n            row_sum += val;\n            pref[base_i + (j + 1)] = row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Vertical accumulation (parallel over columns)\n    #pragma omp parallel for\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n; ++j) {\n        long long cum = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n            pref[i * np1 + j] += pref[(i - 1) * np1 + j];\n        }\n    }\n\n    const int B = 64;\n    long long mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\n\n    // Tiled search, parallel over tile rows\n    #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max : mxSum)\n    for (int ii = 0; ii < n - s + 1; ii += B) {\n        const int i_end = std::min(ii + B, n - s + 1);\n        for (int jj = 0; jj < n - s + 1; jj += B) {\n            const int j_end = std::min(jj + B, n - s + 1);\n            for (int i = ii; i < i_end; ++i) {\n                const long long * __restrict__ row_i_s = &pref[(i + s) * np1];\n                const long long * __restrict__ row_i   = &pref[i * np1];\n                #pragma ivdep\n                for (int j = jj; j < j_end; ++j) {\n                    long long sum = row_i_s[j + s] - row_i[j + s]\n                                   - row_i_s[j] + row_i[j];\n                    if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", static_cast<int>(mxSum));\n    return 0;\n}\n\nThis integrates:\n- Fast integer reader from A.\n- On-the-fly row-wise prefix sums during input from A.\n- Vertical accumulation parallelized with OpenMP from B (but using flat array).\n- Tiling and restrict/ivdep from A.\n- Parallel reduction over tile rows (ii) to utilize multiple cores. This is a new addition not exactly in any source but inspired by the need for parallelism.\n\nWe also use long long as in B/C.\n\nNow, we must specify transformations: list of strings describing what we did. Also sources_kept: all three IDs since we used ideas from each.\n\nHypothesis: \"Integrating fast integer input (source A), on-the-fly row-wise prefix sum construction during input (A), OpenMP-parallelized vertical accumulation pass (B), cache-blocked search with restrict pointers and ivdep pragma (A), and parallel reduction over tile rows to exploit multi-core parallelism yields a correct, high-performance solution that outperforms any single-source variant on the target ARM64 platform.\"\n\nNotes: